WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.720
Did you think your data that was seen by a GPT-3&nbsp;
like model is safely compressed by it? Hidden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.960
among immense amounts of other data?
Then think again.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.920
Is this the end of privacy?

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.360
Hm, hm, Ms. Coffee Bean,&nbsp;
did we not forget something?&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.520
Let’s say hello first, how about that? Oh, sure!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.600
Hello and welcome to this AI&nbsp;
Coffee Break, where we, we, we...

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.120
Where we discuss how it has been possible&nbsp;
by a huge collaboration of big corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:43.840
and university players, to extract training&nbsp;
data from large language models like GPT-2.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.960
Large language models of billions of parameters&nbsp;
are here to stay, just think about BERT, GPT-2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:59.360
and GPT-3 recently, that made a lot of headlines&nbsp;
[whisper] and made it into a Coffee Break video.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.080
And it is quite common that these (usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.480
transformer-based) language models&nbsp;
are trained on private datasets.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.640
So this paper is telling us that they developed&nbsp;
an adversary privacy attack that can extract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.960
verbatim training data from their models.
Like names, phone numbers, addresses or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.040
unique identifiers.
Oh no.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:29.360
In this case, we want to know why&nbsp;
it is possible, how bad this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.880
and what this would mean&nbsp;
for models other than GPT-2.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.800
But first, a little summary about language models:
A language model is a model that predicts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:44.160
next token given the previous ones. The&nbsp;
best example for this is the GPT family.&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.880
For generating text with these&nbsp;
models, one has to input some prefix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.160
which is a sequence of tokens and let the model&nbsp;
predict the next tokens to complete the sequence.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.640
And this is what this paper&nbsp;
does with GPT-2, trained from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.000
public data scraped from the internet.
But bear in mind, the data on the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:10.160
is public, but it is OpenAIs secret which&nbsp;
public data was included in the training set.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.360
This secrecy not wonderful?&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.360
Ok, joke aside:
this makes GPT-2 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:22.880
perfect test case, because the authors can&nbsp;
attack the model and extract data from it,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.760
and search whether the extracted&nbsp;
data can be found on the internet.&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.640
If yes, it was most likely included in&nbsp;
the scraped training set of the model.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.440
But why is it possible to extract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.680
verbatim training instances from&nbsp;
language models in the first place?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.600
Because language models are&nbsp;
memorizing things. If you prompt GPT-2&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.640
to complete the sentence “My address&nbsp;
is 1 Main Street, San Francisco CA”,&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.200
it generates the correct zip code&nbsp;
for San Francisco, California.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.800
And this kind of memorization is good, especially&nbsp;
with things that have no pattern attached to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.880
like unique identifiers, person&nbsp;
names, addresses, phone numbers.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.000
Pattern extraction is what language&nbsp;
models usually do: The majority of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.880
model’s weights cluster data points that&nbsp;
correspond to a common pattern together.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.720
The spare weights -- if there&nbsp;
are some -- model the outliers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.880
the unique points with no pattern that are left&nbsp;
out of the cluster and memorized as they are.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.480
And this memorization is good to capture&nbsp;
things like zip codes but become problematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.040
when things appear only once or twice&nbsp;
during training and get memorized.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:44.960
For this, the authors define the bad type&nbsp;
of memorization: the k-eidetic memorization.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:51.280
In simple terms it just means that a string&nbsp;
was k-eidetic memorized if it occurred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.400
k times in the training data and is&nbsp;
extractable from the language model.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.520
Under this definition, it is&nbsp;
ok for the model to memorize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.480
things occurring 1000 of times in the&nbsp;
training data, but bad if k is small.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:08.320
Now we know that memorization&nbsp;
happens whether we want it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.640
because the model wants to minimize&nbsp;
the loss in the end. So if the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.960
has enough weights left to model outliers and&nbsp;
pattern-less data points, memorization happens.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.760
So how do the authors exploit this&nbsp;
memorization for developing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.120
attack? Their pipeline is quite elegant.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.280
They take GPT-2 and generate&nbsp;
200,000 samples of text with GPT-2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.400
conditioned on all sorts of prefixes.
But these generations are not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.960
memorized pieces of text, they could be anything.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.160
So they sort and select the most&nbsp;
likely pieces to be memorized text.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.640
But the problem here is how to decide&nbsp;
whether the text was memorized or not.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.040
One way to tell is by looking at the&nbsp;
probability of the generated text:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.920
GPT-2 assigns high probability&nbsp;
for memorized strings,&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:05.360
because these data points are outliers,&nbsp;
alone, modelled by their own specific weights.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.680
There is no uncertainty in prediction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:11.360
as there is no neighbor data point&nbsp;
that the model can be mislead by.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.760
And to really be certain that&nbsp;
the high confidence of the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.640
comes from memorization and not from other&nbsp;
regions the model could be certain about,&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.480
the authors compare the probability also to&nbsp;
the output of a smaller version of the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.360
one that does not have the spare&nbsp;
weights to model these outliers.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:38.160
Then, these sorted generations are deduplicated,
the top-100 are chosen,&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.240
and the evaluation is ready to start,&nbsp;
by searching the generated strings on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.880
the internet to check whether they can&nbsp;
be found verbatim on the world-wide-web.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.800
And how successful is this&nbsp;
adversarial privacy attack?&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.240
Well, the situation is worrisome:&nbsp;
for the best variant 67%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.120
of the generated text are word-by-word&nbsp;
repeated snippets from the training data.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.080
Ok, so this was the bad news.
The not that bad news is that&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.720
the generated snippets are&nbsp;
not only named individuals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.120
but also things that are OK,&nbsp;
like licenses or terms of use.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:24.960
The other thing that is not that bad is: this&nbsp;
adversarial attack can not target specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.760
names or contact info. It can just generate things&nbsp;
in bulk and Ms. Coffee Bean just needs to hope it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:34.080
is not about her.
Phew!

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.600
Yet another good bad news is, that even&nbsp;
though the paper makes the effort to define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.760
k-eidetic memorization, the tables do not&nbsp;
include the k that much. And when they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:50.560
like in Table 4, it seems like the&nbsp;
k relates to the document count and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.640
not to the number of occurrences&nbsp;
of the string in each document.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.560
So it seems that for strings&nbsp;
to be memorized by GPT-2 XL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.960
they have to occur more than&nbsp;
30 times in the document.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.480
It is very interesting to see how smaller&nbsp;
versions of GPT-2 need many more string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:16.480
repetitions to memorize, since they have less&nbsp;
of the spare parameters to model outliers.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.200
But this also means that GPT-3, with&nbsp;
100 times more parameters than GPT-2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.280
would need far less repetitions&nbsp;
of strings to memorize them.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.000
And here is where the news is really bad&nbsp;
again. Even though this attack that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.640
have seen is not targeted or tuned&nbsp;
for finding the effective prefixes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.160
from which the language model can&nbsp;
generate the worst memorization,

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.040
the paper also shows an example of how much&nbsp;
better a careful tuning of the prompts can be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:54.480
When completing a prompt with the first digits&nbsp;
of pi, the first 25 correct digits are completed.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:01.520
But when prompted with “pi is”,&nbsp;
the model can output 799 digits,&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:07.200
and with this prompt here, it&nbsp;
can output 824 correct digits.&nbsp;

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.480
How did the authors even come up with this prompt?

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:17.600
Oh, wow. So what to do about it?
The authors propose training with differential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:24.000
privacy, where the long tail containing outliers&nbsp;
prone to memorization is removed from training.&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.720
They also mention curating the training data…&nbsp;
well, this is hard if not impossible to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:37.440
with human manpower, since the data is huuuuge.
Fine-tuning may cause the models to forget the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:44.160
memorization, but might introduce new ones.&nbsp;
Then auditing the models for memorization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.160
seems a good practice and here is&nbsp;
where this paper fits very well.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:53.920
But it is kind of crazy that it took so long&nbsp;
for a study on these models to come out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.400
especially since this memorization&nbsp;
issue was known for LSTMs already.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.280
For all the juicy details, read the&nbsp;
paper, linked in the description below.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:07.200
Do you also like the summary of&nbsp;
contributions at the end of the paper?

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.480
Ms. Coffee Bean would love to see this more&nbsp;
often. Let us know in the comments what you think.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.520
Still here? Now go, read the paper.
Or some other paper.&nbsp;

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:24.280
Or anything else you enjoy. Like watching&nbsp;
another Coffee Break video, hiii!

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:33.360
Okay, bye!

